    "question": {
      ":question/id": "df2da9ea-72e5-5b4e-9c1f-f7932b0d6173",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=e^{t} - 3 e^{- t},\\,y=3 t e^{t},\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=e^{t} + 3 e^{- t},\\,y'(t)=\\left(3 t + 3\\right) e^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(\\left(3 t + 3\\right) e^{t}\\right)}{\\left(e^{t} + 3 e^{- t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 t + 3}{1 + 3 e^{- 2 t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right) + 3}{1 + 3 e^{- 2 \\left(0\\right)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
