      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{5 \\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{e^{t}}{5 t},\\,0<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- \\frac{1}{10 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}},\\,y'(t)=\\frac{5 t e^{t} - 5 e^{t}}{25 t^{2}}=\\frac{\\left(t - 1\\right) e^{t}}{5 t^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(t - 1\\right) e^{t}}{5 t^{2}}\\cdot \\left(- 10 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\left(t - 1\\right) e^{t}}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{2 \\left(\\left(2\\right) - 1\\right) e^{\\left(2\\right)}}{\\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{- 2 e^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =- \\sqrt{2} e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
